<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ac3forge's /analyze rule set: NativeRecommendedRules minus the rules
  whose entire history in this repository is dismissed false positives, or
  fires exclusively on generated (non-project) code.

  Alert identity in GitHub Code Scanning is ruleId + a line-content hash +
  the FILE PATH, so a false positive is not dismissed once: every file
  move re-mints the alert with a fresh ID and the dismissal (a manual API
  call, 280-character comment limit) has to be repeated - exactly what
  happened when PR #291 extracted apps/cli/commands/analysis.cpp out of
  apps/cli/main.cpp and alerts #83-94 resurrected findings already
  documented and dismissed under #70/#71/#8/#11/#22. Neither rule has a
  portable in-source suppression (#pragma warning(suppress:) breaks
  -Werror on GCC/clang, and [[gsl::suppress]] only covers C26xxx and
  trips GCC's -Wattributes), so they are turned off at the source instead.
  A third rule below is disabled for an unrelated reason - it fires only on
  generated code, never on project source:

    C26829 (unwrapped possibly-empty optional) - 7 alerts, all dismissed
      as false positives at sites where a have_first/non-empty-stream
      guard proves the optional engaged (see the comments at each site).
      The same defect class stays covered portably by the nightly clang-tidy job's
      bugprone-unchecked-optional-access on ci.yml's static-analysis leg,
      which CAN be suppressed in-source with a NOLINT where the analysis
      is proven wrong - those NOLINTs and their justifications already
      exist at every affected site.

    C28020 (expression not satisfied in this call) - 2 alerts, both false
      positives on index arithmetic the surrounding code bounds (see
      src/forge/src/oba/atmos.cpp's own comment). Bounds errors stay
      covered by the linux-llvm-asan-ubsan leg (precise, at runtime) and
      CodeQL's dataflow queries.

    C26495 (uninitialized member variable) - 1750 alerts opened in a single
      burst on 2026-09-17, 100% under
      build/config-windows-msvc/apps/gui/.rcc/qmlcache/ac3gui_qmltests_qml/:
      qmlcachegen's generated glue for the GUI's QML tests, one
      compiler-synthesized Storage struct per QML binding closure, not
      project source (see issue #718). ac3gui_qmltests is include()'d into
      apps/gui's own CMake directory scope (apps/gui/CMakeLists.txt has the
      comment explaining why), so this action's directory-scoped
      ignoredTargetPaths can't cleanly separate the test target from the
      production ac3gui target without also blinding analysis to real GUI
      source. No first-party finding has ever been seen under this rule;
      the same defect class stays covered by clang-tidy's
      cppcoreguidelines-pro-type-member-init on the nightly static-analysis
      leg and CodeQL's cpp/uninitialized-local, both of which run against
      project source only.

  C6262 (excessive stack usage) stays ON: it is this workflow's workhorse,
  with 25+ real heap-allocation fixes behind it and no clang-tidy
  equivalent - a genuine finding there gets FIXED, not dismissed, so the
  path-move problem never applies to it.

  Consumed by .github/workflows/msvc-analysis.yml. The Include below
  resolves because the msvc-code-analysis-action passes
  /analyze:rulesetdirectory for Visual Studio's own rule-set directory
  precisely so a repository rule set can layer on an official one.
-->
<RuleSet Name="ac3forge" Description="NativeRecommendedRules minus rules with a false-positive-only history in this repository" ToolsVersion="17.0">
  <Include Path="NativeRecommendedRules.ruleset" Action="Default" />
  <Rules AnalyzerId="Microsoft.Analyzers.NativeCodeAnalysis" RuleNamespace="Microsoft.Rules.Native">
    <Rule Id="C26829" Action="None" />
    <Rule Id="C28020" Action="None" />
    <Rule Id="C26495" Action="None" />
  </Rules>
</RuleSet>
